    "evidence_text": "There were two subtasks.\n\nSLC is a sentence-level binary classification task asking to detect the sentences that contain propaganda.\n\nIntroduction\tPropaganda aims at influencing people's mindset with the purpose of advancing a specific agenda.\n\nIn the Internet era, thanks to the mechanism of sharing in social networks, propaganda campaigns have the potential of reaching very large audiences , , .\n\nWhereas proving intent is not easy, we can analyse the language of a claim/article and look for the use of specific propaganda techniques.\n\nThe task is based on a corpus of news articles annotated with an inventory of 18 propagandist techniques at the fragment level.\n\nWe hope that the corpus would raise interest outside of the community of researchers studying propaganda.\n\nRelated Work\tPropaganda has been tackled mostly at the article level.\n\ncreated a corpus of news articles labelled as propaganda, trusted, hoax, or satire.\n\nexperimented with a binarized version of\n\nthat corpus: propaganda vs. the other three categories.\n\nA related field is that of computational argumentation which, among others, deals with some logical fallacies related to propaganda.\n\npresented a corpus of Web forum discussions with instances of ad hominem fallacy.\n\nUnlike , , , our corpus uses 18 techniques annotated on the same set of news articles.\n\nthe one used for this shared task.\n\nPropaganda Techniques\tPropaganda uses psychological and rhetorical techniques to achieve its objective.\n\nSuch techniques include the use of logical fallacies and appeal to emotions.\n\nWe refer the reader to  for more details on the propaganda techniques; below we report the list of techniques:\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 1.\n\nLoaded language.\n\nUsing words/phrases with strong emotional implications (positive or negative) to influence an audience .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 2.\n\nName calling or labeling.\n\nLabeling the object of the propaganda as something the target audience fears, hates, finds undesirable or otherwise loves or praises .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 3.\n\nRepetition.\n\nRepeating the same message over and over again, so that the audience will eventually accept it , .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 4.\n\nExaggeration or minimization.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 5.\n\nDoubt.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 6.\n\nAppeal to fear/prejudice.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 7.\n\nFlag-waving.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 8.\n\nCausal oversimplification.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 9.\n\nSlogans.\n\nSlogans tend to act as emotional appeals .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 10.\n\nAppeal to authority.\n\nWe include the special case where the reference is not an\n\nauthority/expert, although it is referred to as testimonial in the literature .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 11.\n\nBlack-and-white fallacy, dictatorship.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 12.\n\nThought-terminating clich\u00e9.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 13.\n\nWhataboutism.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 14.\n\nReductio ad Hitlerum.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 15.\n\nRed herring.\n\naway from the points made .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 16.\n\nBandwagon.\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 17.\n\nUsing deliberately unclear words, to let the audience have its own interpretation , .\n\nPropaganda Techniques ::: 18.\n\nStraw man.\n\nGiven a news article, detect all spans of the text in which a propaganda technique is used.\n\nIn addition, for each span the propaganda technique applied must be identified.\n\n(SLC).\n\nA sentence is considered propagandist if it contains at least one propagandist fragment.\n\nFigure FIGREF15 shows an annotated example, which contains several propaganda techniques.\n\nand Labeling.\n\nEvaluation ::: FLC task.\n\n2,538 submissions.\n\nBelow, we give an overview of the approaches as described in the participants' papers.",
